# The server

The server renders every page of the site to HTML on the way to the browser, so a reader — and a crawler — sees the page before the client boots. It is a small Effect HTTP server started from a Bun runtime edge. What passes to the client is the model, inlined for hydration to read.

Rendering every route matters because the site navigates by ordinary links. A nav entry carries a real `href`, so a click leaves the page and asks the server for the next one, and a reader who reloads or arrives from outside asks for a route directly. A server that answered only the landing would give every other page a not-found.

There is one server and one way it works. It reads the shell and the assets from `dist`, renders whichever route a request names, and serves the bundle beside it. Development runs this same file against that same directory, so nothing about answering a request differs between a developer's machine and a reader's. What differs is only who writes `dist`: a build, or a build left watching.

That is worth more than it sounds. A server that renders one way and is developed another agrees with itself only in what a page finally says, and disagrees in everything about how the page arrives — which stylesheet, in which order, before or after the body is visible. Running one path twice settles all of it at once.

The server stays clear of the client runtime. It renders through Foldkit's server module, which runs the view once over a model and writes the result into the shell, and it never starts the runtime the browser boots. The browser hydrates and takes over.

## What the server draws on

From `effect` come `Effect`, the `Cache` that keeps each chapter once it is coloured, the `Fiber` that carries the work the server starts without waiting for it, the `FileSystem` that reads the built shell, the `Option` that fills the model's empty fields and carries a path naming no page, the `pipe` that reads a path as a route or a chapter, the `Schema` that parses what the npm registry returns, and the `Context` and `Layer` that define the server and wire it. From `effect/http` come the request and the responses. `@effect/platform-bun` supplies the Bun HTTP server, its file services, and the runtime edge. Foldkit lends `Server`, from its experimental server module, which renders a view to HTML and writes it into the shell.

The site's own modules supply the view and the model, whose schema doubles as the shape of the flags the page carries to the client. The model also lends the `routeOf` function, which turns the path a request names into the route to render. The documentation shell lends `docsChapterAt`, which turns the address of a chapter into the chapter to open, `chapterAt`, which does the same for any corpus, and `sliceOf`, which cuts the part of a corpus one page shows. The model lends the `Corpus` schema, which the woven book is read against, and the `SearchGroup` schema a search answers in. The client never reads an address itself: it boots from the model the server rendered, so the two always agree on which page and which chapter are on show. The search module lends `indexOf`, which builds the index the palette searches, and `searchIn`, which answers a query from it. The colouring module lends `makeHighlighter`, which loads Shiki, and `colouredCorpus`, which colours the code in a slice of the book.

=>

import { Cache, Context, Effect, Fiber, FileSystem, Layer, Option, pipe, Schema as S } from 'effect'
import { HttpServer, HttpServerRequest, HttpServerResponse } from 'effect/http'
import { BunHttpServer, BunRuntime, BunServices } from '@effect/platform-bun'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { Server } from 'foldkit/experimental'
import { view } from './view'
import { Corpus, Model, routeOf, SearchGroup, type Route } from './model'
import { chapterAt, docsChapterAt, sliceOf } from './docs-page'
import { indexOf, searchIn } from './search'
import { colouredCorpus, makeHighlighter } from './colour'

const distDir = join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..', 'dist')

## The page

The page starts from the built shell and gains three things: the rendered body in place of the empty `#root`, the route's own title in place of the shell's, and, beside the body, the model it was rendered from, which the client reads back to hydrate.

The seed is the site at rest, and every page starts from it — no chapter opened, no section jumped to, the example standing at its first frame, no slice of the book, and the version at the workspace's own until the registry names the latest. It names the landing's route because a model must name one; each page then overrides that single field with its own.

{{ version = ${version} }}

=>

const seed: Model = {
  route: 'home',
  chapter: '',
  drawerOpen: false,
  runtime: 'bun',
  searchOpen: false,
  caret: 0,
  searching: false,
  found: [],
  activeSection: '',
  player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
  version: '::[version]',
  query: '',
  focus: 0,
  copied: '',
  book: Option.none(),
}

## The latest release

The pill and the title bar both name the version, and that version is whatever npm serves as the latest `@athrio/loom` release. The server asks the registry once, when it starts, so a new release reaches the page with the next deploy and no change to the code. The registry answers with the release's metadata, and the `Release` schema keeps the one field the page needs, the version.

The registry may answer late, or not at all. If the server cannot reach it, or it has not answered within two seconds, the `latestVersion` function returns the fallback instead. The timeout also cancels the request. The `Effect.tryPromise` function hands the fetch a signal, which aborts when the timeout interrupts the effect. So the version never fails for want of the network, and never takes more than two seconds to settle.

=>

const NPM_LATEST = 'https://registry.npmjs.org/@athrio/loom/latest'

const Release = S.Struct({ version: S.String })

const latestVersion = (fallback: string): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Effect.tryPromise((signal) =>
    fetch(NPM_LATEST, { signal })
      .then((response) => response.json())
      .then((body) => S.decodeUnknownSync(Release)(body).version),
  ).pipe(
    Effect.timeout('2 seconds'),
    Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(fallback)),
  )

## Loom's own book

The source page draws Loom's own book, and the server is where the book lives. The site's build weaves `corpus/book.loom` to `source.woven.json` beside this file, and the server reads it once, when it starts. The book changes only when the site is built again, so there is nothing to gain from reading it per request, and two megabytes of JSON are worth parsing only once.

The server parses the file and decodes it against the model's `Corpus` schema, which also drops what the shell never draws, such as where each block came from in its loom. A file that is missing or does not decode leaves the server without a book. It still serves every other page, and the source page's addresses then name no page.

=>

const bookFile = join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), 'source.woven.json')

const decodeBook = S.decodeUnknownOption(Corpus)

const readBook = (fs: FileSystem.FileSystem): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<Corpus>> =>
  fs.readFileString(bookFile).pipe(
    Effect.map((text) =>
      Option.flatMap(Option.liftThrowable((json: string): unknown => JSON.parse(json))(text), decodeBook),
    ),
    Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(Option.none())),
  )

## Rendering one page

One model renders one page. The server hands Foldkit the same three things the client boots from: the model's schema as the shape of the flags, an `init` that returns the model it is given, and the view. `Server.renderToString` runs the view over that model and returns the body and the title, with the model serialized beside the body. `Server.injectIntoTemplate` then writes both into the shell, the body over the empty `#root` and the title over the shell's own.

=>

const application = {
  Flags: Model,
  init: (model: Model) => ({ model }),
  view,
}

~

Foldkit stamps the page with a build id, and the client refuses a page whose id differs from its own. The id is the path of the entry script the shell loads. Vite names that file after a hash of its contents, so the path changes with every build that changes the client, and the client reads the same path from its own address. A shell without an entry script is not a build the server can serve, so the render dies rather than stamping a page no client could ever boot.

=>

const ENTRY = /<script type="module"[^>]*\ssrc="([^"]+)"/

const entryOf = (shell: string): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(ENTRY.exec(shell)?.[1]), {
    onNone: () => Effect.die(new Error('the shell loads no entry script')),
    onSome: Effect.succeed,
  })

const pageOf = (shell: string, model: Model): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const buildId = yield* entryOf(shell)
    const rendered = yield* Server.renderToString(application, { flags: model, buildId })
    return Server.injectIntoTemplate(shell, rendered)
  }).pipe(Effect.orDie)

~

The search index reads the site's own pages as their rendered bodies, so `bodyOf` renders a route's page and hands back only its body. That body never reaches a browser, so it needs no shell, and any build id will do.

=>

const bodyOf =
  (version: string) =>
  (route: Route): Effect.Effect<string> =>
    Server.renderToString(application, { flags: { ...seed, route, version }, buildId: 'search' }).pipe(
      Effect.map((rendered) => rendered.html),
      Effect.orDie,
    )

## The pages the server writes itself

Two answers are pages the server writes on its own: the one for an address the site does not have, and the one for a `dist` that has not been built yet.

Neither is built on the shell. The missing page cannot be, because the shell boots the client, and the client hydrates only a page the server rendered for it. Handed a shell with no model in it, Foldkit refuses the page and locks it. The unbuilt page cannot be either, for the plainer reason that the shell is the very thing absent.

So they stand alone and share a shape — centred on the site's paper, set in the site's type, one line of prose and nothing waiting on a bundle.

=>

const PAGE_STYLE = `
      :root { --paper: #F7F4EC; --ink: #14130F; --ink-3: #7C6F64; --rule: #E4DCCB; }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        min-height: 100vh;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 24px;
        padding: 40px;
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font-family: 'Lilex', 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
        text-align: center;
      }
      .number { font-size: 46px; letter-spacing: 0.02em; }
      .headline { font-size: 24px; letter-spacing: 0.01em; }
      .line { margin: 0; max-width: 52ch; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink-3); }
      .line code { color: var(--ink); }
      .back {
        color: var(--ink);
        text-decoration: none;
        padding-bottom: 3px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }
      .back:hover { color: var(--ink-3); }
`

const standalone = (title: string, body: string): string => `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>${title}</title>
    <style>${PAGE_STYLE}</style>
  </head>
  <body>${body}
  </body>
</html>
`

~

The missing page carries the mark, the number, a sentence, and the way back. A reader who has just missed a page waits on nothing to be told so, and the same page answers in development and in production alike.

=>

const MISSING = standalone(
  'Not found — Loom',
  `
    <img src="/mark.svg" alt="Loom" width="140" height="60" />
    <div class="number">404</div>
    <p class="line">There is no page at this address.</p>
    <a class="back" href="/">Back to Loom</a>`,
)

const notFound = Effect.succeed(
  HttpServerResponse.text(MISSING, { status: 404, contentType: 'text/html' }),
)

~

The unbuilt page carries no mark, since the mark is one of the files that has not been written. It names the command that fixes the matter, which costs a line and saves the reader working out why a directory is empty.

=>

const NOT_BUILT = standalone(
  'Not built — Loom',
  `
    <div class="headline">The site is not built</div>
    <p class="line">The server renders from <code>dist</code>, and nothing is there yet. Run <code>bun run build</code>, or <code>bun run dev</code> to keep it building.</p>`,
)

## Read fresh for every page

The site reads its shell for each page rather than holding one from startup, because `dist` is rewritten underneath a running server every time a build finishes. A shell held from startup would go on naming the bundle the first build wrote. The page would then arrive running code a developer has since changed, or pointing at files a later build has removed. Reading it per request costs one small file and keeps the server honest about whatever `dist` holds at that moment.

Rendering per request follows, and costs about as little: a render is one pass of the view and one write into the shell. Four things are made once rather than per request: the latest release, the book, the search index and the highlighter. A version changes on a release rather than on a request. The book, and the index built from it, change only with a build. The highlighter loads its grammars once rather than for every page.

Of those four, the server waits only for the book before it is ready to answer. Every request needs the book, since the book decides which `/source` addresses name a page, and reading it takes a few tens of milliseconds. The other three take far longer. The registry can take a second to answer, and the index most of a second to build. A development server restarts on every tangle, and a developer waits out each restart before the page will load.

So the server sets the registry and the highlighter going and does not wait for them. It starts each in a fiber, a piece of work that runs beside the rest of the program. The `Fiber.join` function waits for a fiber's result, or hands it straight back once the fiber has finished. Each page joins the `latest` fiber for its version, and a source page joins the `highlighter` fiber before its chapter is coloured. Only a request in the first two seconds after a start can find either fiber still running, and that request waits for it rather than answering without it.

The server leaves the search index for last. It builds the index when the first search asks for it, and the `Effect.cached` combinator keeps the index for every search after. Building the index occupies the server for most of a second, and no other request is answered in that time. Built at startup, the index would stall the very pages a developer reloads after a change.

Two answers are the whole of what the site owes the handler. The `pageAt` function asks `placeOf` whether the path names a page at all, then renders that page through the shell it has just read. A path names a page in one of three ways. It is a route's own path, such as `/why-loom` or `/docs`, which opens the route as it stands. Or it is a chapter's address under `/docs`, which opens the documentation on that chapter. Or it is a chapter's address under `/source`, which opens the book on that chapter, when the server has a book. `/source` itself opens the book's first chapter, so it too names a page only when the server has a book. Without one it would render the page's frame around nothing: the nav, an empty column and the footer. Any other path names no page, so the server looks for an asset, and answers with the missing page when there is none. A `dist` it cannot read yields the unbuilt page. A render that fails is a fault in the site rather than in the build, so it surfaces as one instead of hiding behind the unbuilt page. The `assetAt` function reads a file from `dist` — the client bundle, the stylesheet, the marks — and falls back to the missing page when there is no such file. Both take the request's whole URL, since a path alone is what the handler happens to match on rather than all it holds.

A source page also carries its slice of the book in its model: the book's table of contents and the one chapter the place names, with its code in colour. Colouring the whole book at startup would hold the server up for seconds, for chapters a reader may never open before the next build. So `slices` colours a chapter the first time a page asks for it, and keeps the coloured slice for every request after. The cache holds one slice for each chapter, and one more for the book's base address, which opens on its first chapter. `bookFor` asks the cache for a source page's slice, and every other page carries none.

The site answers one more thing, a search. `searchAt` reads the query from the request's `q` parameter and answers with the groups `searchIn` finds, encoded through the `SearchGroup` schema, so the client reads back exactly the shape the server meant.

=>

type Place = { readonly route: Route; readonly chapter: string }

const placeOf = (book: Option.Option<Corpus>, pathname: string): Option.Option<Place> =>
  pipe(
    routeOf(pathname),
    Option.filter((route) => route !== 'source' || Option.isSome(book)),
    Option.map((route): Place => ({ route, chapter: '' })),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Option.map(docsChapterAt(pathname), (chapter): Place => ({ route: 'docs', chapter })),
    ),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Option.flatMap(book, (corpus) =>
        Option.map(
          chapterAt(corpus, '/source', pathname),
          (chapter): Place => ({ route: 'source', chapter }),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

export class Site extends Context.Service<Site>()('Site', {
  make: Effect.gen(function* () {
    const fs = yield* FileSystem.FileSystem
    const book = yield* readBook(fs)
    const latest = yield* Effect.forkScoped(latestVersion(seed.version))
    const highlighter = yield* Effect.forkScoped(makeHighlighter)
    const index = yield* Effect.cached(
      Effect.flatMap(Fiber.join(latest), (version) => indexOf(book, bodyOf(version))),
    )
    const slices = yield* Cache.make({
      capacity: Option.match(book, { onNone: () => 1, onSome: (corpus) => corpus.pages.length + 1 }),
      lookup: (chapter: string) =>
        Effect.map(Fiber.join(highlighter), (shiki) =>
          Option.map(book, (corpus) => colouredCorpus(shiki, sliceOf(corpus, chapter))),
        ),
    })
    const bookFor = (place: Place): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<Corpus>> =>
      place.route === 'source' ? Cache.get(slices, place.chapter) : Effect.succeed(Option.none())
    const shell = fs.readFileString(join(distDir, 'index.html'))
    return {
      pageAt: (url: URL): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>> =>
        Option.match(placeOf(book, url.pathname), {
          onNone: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none()),
          onSome: (place) =>
            shell.pipe(
              Effect.matchEffect({
                onFailure: () => Effect.succeed(NOT_BUILT),
                onSuccess: (held) =>
                  Effect.gen(function* () {
                    const version = yield* Fiber.join(latest)
                    const slice = yield* bookFor(place)
                    return yield* pageOf(held, { ...seed, ...place, version, book: slice })
                  }),
              }),
              Effect.map(Option.some),
            ),
        }),
      assetAt: (url: URL) =>
        HttpServerResponse.file(join(distDir, url.pathname)).pipe(
          Effect.catchCause(() => notFound),
        ),
      searchAt: (url: URL): Effect.Effect<HttpServerResponse.HttpServerResponse> =>
        Effect.flatMap(index, (entries) =>
          HttpServerResponse.schemaJson(S.Array(SearchGroup))(
            searchIn(entries, url.searchParams.get('q') ?? ''),
          ),
        ).pipe(Effect.orDie),
    } as const
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make).pipe(Layer.provide(BunServices.layer))
}

## Answering a request

The server answers `/search` from the index. For any other path it asks the site for a page at the request URL. A path that names a route returns it as HTML, and anything else is an asset read from `dist`.

=>

const SEARCH = '/search'

const handle = (site: Site['Service'], url: URL) =>
  url.pathname === SEARCH
    ? site.searchAt(url)
    : Effect.flatMap(
        site.pageAt(url),
        Option.match({
          onSome: (page) => Effect.succeed(HttpServerResponse.text(page, { contentType: 'text/html' })),
          onNone: () => site.assetAt(url),
        }),
      )

## Serving it {Tangle}[server.ts]

The handler reads the request and answers it. The server serves that handler over a Bun HTTP layer and launches from the Bun runtime edge. There is nothing here to switch on, which is the point: this file is what a developer runs and what the image runs, and neither reads an environment variable to find out which it is.

The order of the site's layer and the Bun HTTP layer decides when the port opens. The `Layer.provide` function builds the layer it is given before the layer it feeds, so the site, provided last, is built first. The Bun HTTP layer opens the port only after that, once the site can answer. Until the handler is installed, the Bun HTTP layer answers every request itself, with a bare `404` whose body reads `not found`. In the other order that placeholder would answer every request for as long as the site took to start, and a reload during a development restart would show an all but empty page.

=>

::[What the server draws on]

::[The page]

::[The latest release]

::[Loom's own book]

::[Rendering one page]

::[The pages the server writes itself]

::[Read fresh for every page]

::[Answering a request]

const app = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const request = yield* HttpServerRequest.HttpServerRequest
  const site = yield* Site
  return yield* handle(site, new URL(request.url, 'http://localhost'))
})

const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 5199)

const server = HttpServer.serve(app).pipe(
  HttpServer.withLogAddress,
  Layer.provide(BunHttpServer.layer({ port })),
  Layer.provide(Site.layer),
)

BunRuntime.runMain(Layer.launch(server))
